/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba skills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md' in /Users/kakao1/.codex/jstack
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	---
     2	name: test-driven-development
     3	description: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Test-Driven Development (TDD)
     7	
     8	## Overview
     9	
    10	Write the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.
    11	
    12	**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.
    13	
    14	**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**
    15	
    16	## When to Use
    17	
    18	**Always — for logic that passes the test qualification gate in
    19	jstack:simplest-thing (branches, loops, parsers, money/security boundaries,
    20	data-loss paths):**
    21	- New features
    22	- Bug fixes
    23	- Refactoring
    24	- Behavior changes
    25	
    26	Logic that fails the gate (trivial one-liners, glue code, pure delegation)
    27	needs no test. The gate decides with objective criteria, not "feels simple."
    28	
    29	**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**
    30	- Throwaway prototypes
    31	- Generated code
    32	- Configuration files
    33	
    34	Thinking "skip TDD just this once"? Stop. That's rationalization.
    35	
    36	## The Iron Law
    37	
    38	```
    39	NO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST
    40	```
    41	
    42	Scope: this law binds all logic that passes the test qualification gate
    43	(jstack:simplest-thing). Code that does not qualify for a test does not enter
    44	this cycle — but when a test is warranted, it comes first, always.
    45	
    46	Write gate-qualifying code before the test? Delete it. Start over.
    47	
    48	**No exceptions:**
    49	- Don't keep it as "reference"
    50	- Don't "adapt" it while writing tests
    51	- Don't look at it
    52	- Delete means delete
    53	
    54	Implement fresh from tests. Period.
    55	
    56	## Red-Green-Refactor
    57	
    58	```dot
    59	digraph tdd_cycle {
    60	    rankdir=LR;
    61	    red [label="RED\nWrite failing test", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor="#ffcccc"];
    62	    verify_red [label="Verify fails\ncorrectly", shape=diamond];
    63	    green [label="GREEN\nMinimal code", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor="#ccffcc"];
    64	    verify_green [label="Verify passes\nAll green", shape=diamond];
    65	    refactor [label="REFACTOR\nClean up", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor="#ccccff"];
    66	    next [label="Next", shape=ellipse];
    67	
    68	    red -> verify_red;
    69	    verify_red -> green [label="yes"];
    70	    verify_red -> red [label="wrong\nfailure"];
    71	    green -> verify_green;
    72	    verify_green -> refactor [label="yes"];
    73	    verify_green -> green [label="no"];
    74	    refactor -> verify_green [label="stay\ngreen"];
    75	    verify_green -> next;
    76	    next -> red;
    77	}
    78	```
    79	
    80	### RED - Write Failing Test
    81	
    82	Write one minimal test showing what should happen.
    83	
    84	<Good>
    85	```typescript
    86	test('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {
    87	  let attempts = 0;
    88	  const operation = () => {
    89	    attempts++;
    90	    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');
    91	    return 'success';
    92	  };
    93	
    94	  const result = await retryOperation(operation);
    95	
    96	  expect(result).toBe('success');
    97	  expect(attempts).toBe(3);
    98	});
    99	```
   100	Clear name, tests real behavior, one thing
   101	</Good>
   102	
   103	<Bad>
   104	```typescript
   105	test('retry works', async () => {
   106	  const mock = jest.fn()
   107	    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())
   108	    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())
   109	    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');
   110	  await retryOperation(mock);
   111	  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);
   112	});
   113	```
   114	Vague name, tests mock not code
   115	</Bad>
   116	
   117	**Requirements:**
   118	- One behavior
   119	- Clear name
   120	- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)
   121	
   122	### Verify RED - Watch It Fail
   123	
   124	**MANDATORY. Never skip.**
   125	
   126	```bash
   127	npm test path/to/test.test.ts
   128	```
   129	
   130	Confirm:
   131	- Test fails (not errors)
   132	- Failure message is expected
   133	- Fails because feature missing (not typos)
   134	
   135	**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.
   136	
   137	**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.
   138	
   139	### GREEN - Minimal Code
   140	
   141	Write simplest code to pass the test.
   142	
   143	<Good>
   144	```typescript
   145	async function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
   146	  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
   147	    try {
   148	      return await fn();
   149	    } catch (e) {
   150	      if (i === 2) throw e;
   151	    }
   152	  }
   153	  throw new Error('unreachable');
   154	}
   155	```
   156	Just enough to pass
   157	</Good>
   158	
   159	<Bad>
   160	```typescript
   161	async function retryOperation<T>(
   162	  fn: () => Promise<T>,
   163	  options?: {
   164	    maxRetries?: number;
   165	    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';
   166	    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;
   167	  }
   168	): Promise<T> {
   169	  // YAGNI
   170	}
   171	```
   172	Over-engineered
   173	</Bad>
   174	
   175	Don't add features, refactor other code, or "improve" beyond the test.
   176	
   177	### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass
   178	
   179	**MANDATORY.**
   180	
   181	```bash
   182	npm test path/to/test.test.ts
   183	```
   184	
   185	Confirm:
   186	- Test passes
   187	- Other tests still pass
   188	- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)
   189	
   190	**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.
   191	
   192	**Other tests fail?** Fix now.
   193	
   194	### REFACTOR - Clean Up
   195	
   196	After green only:
   197	- Remove duplication
   198	- Improve names
   199	- Extract helpers
   200	
   201	Keep tests green. Don't add behavior.
   202	
   203	### Repeat
   204	
   205	Next failing test for next feature.
   206	
   207	## Good Tests
   208	
   209	| Quality | Good | Bad |
   210	|---------|------|-----|
   211	| **Minimal** | One thing. "and" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |
   212	| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |
   213	| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |
   214	
   215	## Why Order Matters
   216	
   217	Tests written after the code pass immediately — and passing immediately proves nothing: the test might check the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never saw it catch the bug.
   218	
   219	Test-first forces you to watch the test fail, which proves it actually tests something. It also forces edge-case discovery *before* implementing, instead of verifying only the cases you happened to remember.
   220	
   221	Tests-after answer "what does this do?" Tests-first answer "what should this do?" Coverage added after the fact is not TDD — you get the coverage but lose the proof the tests work.
   222	
   223	## Common Rationalizations
   224	
   225	| Excuse | Reality |
   226	|--------|---------|
   227	| "Too simple to test" | Feelings don't decide — the qualification gate does (branch/loop/parse/money/security). Qualifies? Test it. Doesn't? Skip it deliberately. |
   228	| "I'll test after" | Tests passing immediately prove nothing. |
   229	| "Tests after achieve same goals" | Tests-after = "what does this do?" Tests-first = "what should this do?" |
   230	| "Already manually tested" | Ad-hoc ≠ systematic. No record, can't re-run. |
   231	| "Deleting X hours is wasteful" | Sunk cost fallacy. Keeping unverified code is technical debt. |
   232	| "Keep as reference, write tests first" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |
   233	| "Need to explore first" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |
   234	| "Test hard = design unclear" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |
   235	| "TDD will slow me down" | TDD faster than debugging. Pragmatic = test-first. |
   236	| "Manual test faster" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |
   237	| "Existing code has no tests" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |
   238	
   239	## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over
   240	
   241	- Code before test
   242	- Test after implementation
   243	- Test passes immediately
   244	- Can't explain why test failed
   245	- Tests added "later"
   246	- Rationalizing "just this once"
   247	- "I already manually tested it"
   248	- "Tests after achieve the same purpose"
   249	- "It's about spirit not ritual"
   250	- "Keep as reference" or "adapt existing code"
   251	- "Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful"
   252	- "TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic"
   253	- "This is different because..."
   254	
   255	**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**
   256	
   257	## Example: Bug Fix
   258	
   259	**Bug:** Empty email accepted
   260	
   261	**RED**
   262	```typescript
   263	test('rejects empty email', async () => {
   264	  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });
   265	  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');
   266	});
   267	```
   268	
   269	**Verify RED**
   270	```bash
   271	$ npm test
   272	FAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined
   273	```
   274	
   275	**GREEN**
   276	```typescript
   277	function submitForm(data: FormData) {
   278	  if (!data.email?.trim()) {
   279	    return { error: 'Email required' };
   280	  }
   281	  // ...
   282	}
   283	```
   284	
   285	**Verify GREEN**
   286	```bash
   287	$ npm test
   288	PASS
   289	```
   290	
   291	**REFACTOR**
   292	Extract validation for multiple fields if needed.
   293	
   294	## Verification Checklist
   295	
   296	Before marking work complete:
   297	
   298	- [ ] Every gate-qualifying function/method has a test (jstack:simplest-thing gate)
   299	- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing
   300	- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)
   301	- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test
   302	- [ ] All tests pass
   303	- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)
   304	- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)
   305	- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered
   306	
   307	Can't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.
   308	
   309	## When Stuck
   310	
   311	| Problem | Solution |
   312	|---------|----------|
   313	| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |
   314	| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |
   315	| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |
   316	| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |
   317	
   318	## Debugging Integration
   319	
   320	Bug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.
   321	
   322	Never fix bugs without a test.
   323	
   324	## Testing Anti-Patterns
   325	
   326	When adding mocks or test utilities, read @testing-anti-patterns.md to avoid common pitfalls:
   327	- Testing mock behavior instead of real behavior
   328	- Adding test-only methods to production classes
   329	- Mocking without understanding dependencies
   330	
   331	## Final Rule
   332	
   333	```
   334	Production code → test exists and failed first
   335	Otherwise → not TDD
   336	```
   337	
   338	No exceptions without your human partner's permission.

