    61	echo "[PASS] jstack static contract"
     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	# Run all skill triggering tests
     3	# Usage: ./run-all.sh
     4	
     5	set -e
     6	
     7	SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
     8	PROMPTS_DIR="$SCRIPT_DIR/prompts"
     9	
    10	SKILLS=(
    11	    "systematic-debugging"
    12	    "test-driven-development"
    13	    "writing-plans"
    14	    "dispatching-parallel-agents"
    15	    "executing-plans"
    16	    "requesting-code-review"
    17	)
    18	
    19	echo "=== Running Skill Triggering Tests ==="
    20	echo ""
    21	
    22	PASSED=0
    23	FAILED=0
    24	RESULTS=()
    25	
    26	for skill in "${SKILLS[@]}"; do
    27	    prompt_file="$PROMPTS_DIR/${skill}.txt"
    28	
    29	    if [ ! -f "$prompt_file" ]; then
    30	        echo "⚠️  SKIP: No prompt file for $skill"
    31	        continue
    32	    fi
    33	
    34	    echo "Testing: $skill"
    35	
    36	    if "$SCRIPT_DIR/run-test.sh" "$skill" "$prompt_file" 3 2>&1 | tee /tmp/skill-test-$skill.log; then
    37	        PASSED=$((PASSED + 1))
    38	        RESULTS+=("✅ $skill")
    39	    else
    40	        FAILED=$((FAILED + 1))
    41	        RESULTS+=("❌ $skill")
    42	    fi
    43	
    44	    echo ""
    45	    echo "---"
    46	    echo ""
    47	done
    48	
    49	echo ""
    50	echo "=== Summary ==="
    51	for result in "${RESULTS[@]}"; do
    52	    echo "  $result"
    53	done
    54	echo ""
    55	echo "Passed: $PASSED"
    56	echo "Failed: $FAILED"
    57	
    58	if [ $FAILED -gt 0 ]; then
    59	    exit 1
    60	fi
    60	**Split a plan task into smaller slices when:**
    61	- It touches unrelated files or subsystems.
    62	- It has separable tests that can pass independently.
    63	- The implementer would need to hold too many details in context.
    64	- Reviewers would struggle to distinguish spec compliance from code quality.
    65	
    66	**Switch to inline execution instead when:**
    67	- Almost every task depends on hidden state from the previous one.
    68	- The work is diagnosis/debugging where test output changes the next step.
    69	- The environment depends on live services, credentials, long-running processes, or shared local state.
    70	- Slicing would create more coordination cost than implementation cost.
    71	
    72	Record the chosen slices in TodoWrite with enough detail to show why each slice is isolated, grouped, or split.
    73	
    74	When consecutive plan tasks are tagged `parallel-safe` and share no files or state, dispatch them concurrently in one batch via jstack:dispatching-parallel-agents — do not run them sequentially.
    75	
    76	**Checkout altitude:** all slices in this skill share ONE checkout (the current worktree) — subagents parallelize *within* it. Never create additional worktrees per slice; a slice is a commit unit, not a checkout unit. A separate Orca worktree (jstack:using-git-worktrees Step 1a) is only for a genuinely independent plan/feature that deserves its own card and branch.
    77	
    78	**Progress visibility:** if the current checkout is Orca-managed, refresh the card as each slice completes — `orca worktree set --worktree active --comment "slice <n>/<total> done: <slice name>"` — and set `--workspace-status in-review` when entering final review. This is the user's monitoring surface, not a check-in prompt; continuous execution still applies.
    79	
    80	## Review Intensity
    81	
    82	Pick the review gate per slice. Do not skip review; choose the right size.
    15	
    16	## Subagent dispatch requires multi-agent support
    17	
    18	Add to your Codex config (`~/.codex/config.toml`):
    19	
    20	```toml
    21	[features]
    22	multi_agent = true
    23	```
    24	
    25	This enables `spawn_agent`, `wait`, and `close_agent` for skills like `dispatching-parallel-agents` and `subagent-driven-development`.
    26	
    27	## Subagent dispatch
    28	
    29	jstack skills dispatch reviewer/implementer subagents via `Task (general-purpose)`
    30	with an inline prompt (filled from the skill's local template, e.g.
    31	`subagent-driven-development/code-reviewer.md` or `code-quality-reviewer-prompt.md`).
    32	On Codex, route this to `spawn_agent(message=...)` with the filled prompt content.
    33	
    34	### Message framing
    35	
     1	---
     2	name: requesting-code-review
     3	description: Use when completing tasks, implementing major features, or before merging to verify work meets requirements
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Requesting Code Review
     7	
     8	This skill is consolidated into **jstack:peer-review**. Use `jstack:peer-review` with mode `review` (diff against base) or `plan` (spec/plan). peer-review owns reviewer routing (Codex/Claude), the prompt boundary, commands, artifacts, and finding triage. See the `## When to Request` and `## Red Flags` sections there.
     1	---
     2	name: receiving-code-review
     3	description: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Code Review Reception
     7	
     8	## The Response Pattern
     9	
    10	```
    11	WHEN receiving code review feedback:
    12	
    13	1. READ: Complete feedback without reacting
    14	2. UNDERSTAND: Restate requirement in own words (or ask)
    15	3. VERIFY: Check against codebase reality
    16	4. EVALUATE: Technically sound for THIS codebase?
    17	5. RESPOND: Technical acknowledgment or reasoned pushback
    18	6. IMPLEMENT: One item at a time, test each
    19	```
    20	
    21	## Forbidden Responses
    22	
    23	**NEVER:**
    24	- "You're absolutely right!" (explicit CLAUDE.md violation)
    25	- "Great point!" / "Excellent feedback!" (performative)
    26	- "Let me implement that now" (before verification)
    27	
    28	**INSTEAD:**
    29	- Restate the technical requirement
    30	- Ask clarifying questions
    31	- Push back with technical reasoning if wrong
    32	- Just start working (actions > words)
    33	
    34	## Handling Unclear Feedback
    35	
    36	```
    37	IF any item is unclear:
    38	  STOP - do not implement anything yet
    39	  ASK for clarification on unclear items
    40	
    41	WHY: Items may be related. Partial understanding = wrong implementation.
    42	```
    43	
    44	**Example:**
    45	```
    46	your human partner: "Fix 1-6"
    47	You understand 1,2,3,6. Unclear on 4,5.
    48	
    49	❌ WRONG: Implement 1,2,3,6 now, ask about 4,5 later
    50	✅ RIGHT: "I understand items 1,2,3,6. Need clarification on 4 and 5 before proceeding."
    51	```
    52	
    53	## Source-Specific Handling
    54	
    55	### From your human partner
    56	- **Trusted** - implement after understanding
    57	- **Still ask** if scope unclear
    58	- **No performative agreement**
    59	- **Skip to action** or technical acknowledgment
    60	
    61	### From External Reviewers
    62	```
    63	BEFORE implementing:
    64	  1. Check: Technically correct for THIS codebase?
    65	  2. Check: Breaks existing functionality?
    66	  3. Check: Reason for current implementation?
    67	  4. Check: Works on all platforms/versions?
    68	  5. Check: Does reviewer understand full context?
    69	
    70	IF suggestion seems wrong:
    71	  Push back with technical reasoning
    72	
    73	IF can't easily verify:
    74	  Say so: "I can't verify this without [X]. Should I [investigate/ask/proceed]?"
    75	
    76	IF conflicts with your human partner's prior decisions:
    77	  Stop and discuss with your human partner first
    78	```
    79	
    80	**your human partner's rule:** "External feedback - be skeptical, but check carefully"
    81	
    82	## YAGNI Check for "Professional" Features
    83	
    84	```
    85	IF reviewer suggests "implementing properly":
    86	  grep codebase for actual usage
    87	
    88	  IF unused: "This endpoint isn't called. Remove it (YAGNI)?"
    89	  IF used: Then implement properly
    90	```
    91	
    92	**your human partner's rule:** "You and reviewer both report to me. If we don't need this feature, don't add it."
    93	
    94	## Implementation Order
    95	
    96	```
    97	FOR multi-item feedback:
    98	  1. Clarify anything unclear FIRST
    99	  2. Then implement in this order:
   100	     - Blocking issues (breaks, security)
   101	     - Simple fixes (typos, imports)
   102	     - Complex fixes (refactoring, logic)
   103	  3. Test each fix individually
   104	  4. Verify no regressions
   105	```
   106	
   107	## When To Push Back
   108	
   109	Push back when:
   110	- Suggestion breaks existing functionality
   111	- Reviewer lacks full context
   112	- Violates YAGNI (unused feature)
   113	- Technically incorrect for this stack
   114	- Legacy/compatibility reasons exist
   115	- Conflicts with your human partner's architectural decisions
   116	
   117	**How to push back:**
   118	- Use technical reasoning, not defensiveness
   119	- Ask specific questions
   120	- Reference working tests/code
   121	- Involve your human partner if architectural
   122	
   123	**Signal if uncomfortable pushing back out loud:** "Strange things are afoot at the Circle K"
   124	
   125	## Acknowledging Correct Feedback
   126	
   127	When feedback IS correct:
   128	```
   129	✅ "Fixed. [Brief description of what changed]"
   130	✅ "Good catch - [specific issue]. Fixed in [location]."
   131	✅ [Just fix it and show in the code]
   132	
   133	❌ "You're absolutely right!"
   134	❌ "Great point!"
   135	❌ "Thanks for catching that!"
   136	❌ "Thanks for [anything]"
   137	❌ ANY gratitude expression
   138	```
   139	
   140	**Why no thanks:** Actions speak. Just fix it. The code itself shows you heard the feedback.
   141	
   142	**If you catch yourself about to write "Thanks":** DELETE IT. State the fix instead.
   143	
   144	## Gracefully Correcting Your Pushback
   145	
   146	If you pushed back and were wrong:
   147	```
   148	✅ "You were right - I checked [X] and it does [Y]. Implementing now."
   149	✅ "Verified this and you're correct. My initial understanding was wrong because [reason]. Fixing."
   150	
   151	❌ Long apology
   152	❌ Defending why you pushed back
   153	❌ Over-explaining
   154	```
   155	
   156	State the correction factually and move on.
   157	
   158	## Common Mistakes
   159	
   160	| Mistake | Fix |
   161	|---------|-----|
   162	| Performative agreement | State requirement or just act |
   163	| Blind implementation | Verify against codebase first |
   164	| Batch without testing | One at a time, test each |
   165	| Assuming reviewer is right | Check if breaks things |
   166	| Avoiding pushback | Technical correctness > comfort |
   167	| Partial implementation | Clarify all items first |
   168	| Can't verify, proceed anyway | State limitation, ask for direction |
   169	
   170	## Real Examples
   171	
   172	**Performative Agreement (Bad):**
   173	```
   174	Reviewer: "Remove legacy code"
   175	❌ "You're absolutely right! Let me remove that..."
   176	```
   177	
   178	**Technical Verification (Good):**
   179	```
   180	Reviewer: "Remove legacy code"
   181	✅ "Checking... build target is 10.15+, this API needs 13+. Need legacy for backward compat. Current impl has wrong bundle ID - fix it or drop pre-13 support?"
   182	```
   183	
   184	**YAGNI (Good):**
   185	```
   186	Reviewer: "Implement proper metrics tracking with database, date filters, CSV export"
   187	✅ "Grepped codebase - nothing calls this endpoint. Remove it (YAGNI)? Or is there usage I'm missing?"
   188	```
   189	
   190	**Unclear Item (Good):**
   191	```
   192	your human partner: "Fix items 1-6"
   193	You understand 1,2,3,6. Unclear on 4,5.
   194	✅ "Understand 1,2,3,6. Need clarification on 4 and 5 before implementing."
   195	```
   196	
   197	## GitHub Thread Replies
   198	
   199	When replying to inline review comments on GitHub, reply in the comment thread (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments/{id}/replies`), not as a top-level PR comment.
