     1	---
     2	name: peer-review
     3	description: Use when an independent outside review is needed for a diff, plan, spec, security-sensitive change, live-risk workflow, or adversarial challenge.
     4	---
     5	
     6	# Peer Review
     7	
     8	Get the opposite primary agent to review the work. In Codex, ask Claude. In Claude
     9	Code, ask Codex. The reviewer is read-only and advisory. The active agent evaluates
    10	findings before applying anything.
    11	
    12	## Modes
    13	
    14	- `review`: review the current diff against the base branch.
    15	- `challenge`: adversarial review. Look for production failure modes, race
    16	  conditions, security holes, resource leaks, and silent data corruption.
    17	- `plan`: review a spec or implementation plan for blocking issues.
    18	- `consult`: ask a focused question about the repo.
    19	- `complexity`: over-engineering hunt. Finds what to delete: reinvented
    20	  standard library, unneeded dependencies, speculative abstractions, dead
    21	  flexibility. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it.
    22	
    23	Default to `review` when there is a diff. Default to `plan` when the user points at
    24	a spec or plan. Use `challenge` for money movement, auth, security, exchange
    25	adapters, state machines, live-smoke paths, and release blockers. Use `complexity`
    26	when the user asks what can be deleted, simplified, or whether something is
    27	over-engineered.
    28	
    29	## When to Request
    30	
    31	Request an outside review when: completing a task or major feature, before merging, or before any security/auth/payments/live-risk change. Reviewing your own work in the same context is not a substitute — the point is an independent lane.
    32	
    33	## Red Flags (review-avoidance rationalizations)
    34	
    35	| Thought | Reality |
    36	|---|---|
    37	| "It's a small change, skip review" | Small diffs hide the costliest bugs. Request it. |
    38	| "I already checked it myself" | Self-review in the authoring context is not independent. |
    39	| "Tests pass, so it's fine" | Tests prove what you thought to test, not what you missed. |
    40	| "Review will slow me down" | A closed PR slows you down more. |
    41	
    42	## Host Routing
    43	
    44	Use the opposite reviewer:
    45	
    46	- Running in Codex: call local Claude Code CLI with `claude -p`.
    47	- Running in Claude Code: call local Codex CLI with `codex review` or `codex exec`.
    48	- If host is unclear, infer from available runtime context. If still unclear and
    49	  both CLIs exist, prefer the reviewer not already driving the current session.
    50	
    51	Do not ask both reviewers by default. The point is independence, not consensus theater.
    52	
    53	## Prompt Boundary
    54	
    55	Prefix every outside-review prompt with:
   228	```
   229	
   230	Show raw reviewer output first, then a short triage. Do not implement accepted
   231	fixes unless the workflow has reached a step where edits are allowed.
   232	
   233	## Review Report
   234	
   235	When a spec, plan, or CURRENT file is active, add or update:
   236	
   237	```markdown
   238	## JSTACK REVIEW REPORT
   239	
   240	| Check | Reviewer | Runs | Status | Findings | Artifact |
   241	|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
   242	| Peer Review | <codex|claude> | 1 | <Pass|Issues Found> | <summary> | <path> |
   243	| Adversarial Review | <codex|claude> | 0 | Pending | - | - |
   244	```
   245	
   246	Preserve existing rows. Append missing rows. If no active file is obvious, only
   247	write the artifact and mention where it was saved.
   248	
   249	## Finding Triage
   250	
   251	External feedback is not an order. Before applying it:
   252	
   253	1. Verify the finding against code, tests, logs, or docs.
   254	2. Reject findings that contradict repo invariants or add unused scope.
   255	3. Escalate product, architecture, live-risk, or funding-impact decisions.
   256	4. Apply accepted fixes one at a time with targeted tests.
   257	
   258	For live trading, movement, signer, exchange, deployment, or credential changes,
   259	do not proceed from review to live execution without explicit user confirmation.
224:## Triage
230:Show raw reviewer output first, then a short triage. Do not implement accepted
